ared  okay so as a summary of what happened  here we started with the fact that the  log of the volume of the polar of a  given polytope is a concave function  as a as a consequence the hessian of  that function has exactly one positive  eigenvalue  which implies that uh you are using  using then a relationship with a  laplacian matrix we can show that that  matrix has a sufficiently large spectral  gap such that when we apply  a theorem  from spectral graph theory that you we  arrive at the fact that the graph has  small diameter  okay  that's not summary of uh these results  and some open questions uh in the future  are  one is can we prove the polynomial horse  conjecture for random polytopes um  so we believe the general polynomial  herc conjecture is still completely open  and  second can we analyze what happens for a  random walk on the graph so this was  analyzing sort of the  the length of an optimal walk  but what happens for a random walk  and the third question is can we use  some higher hard riemann relations uh  for polytopes so the fact that the  hessian has exactly one positive  eigenvalue is a dream on relation but  there's a question as to whether a kind  of highly speculative idea as to whether  the higher relations could do something  as well  um that's it for the talk i appreciate  you all uh tuning in and feel free to  email us if you have any questions or  uh thoughts on these open questions  and thank you and have a great  conference
 

